---
title: "Reading surface"
---

This page is a reading surface. It is lit by the reader's own device: a phone set to dark
renders it dark, a laptop set to light renders it light, and it keeps following that setting
live if the reader changes it mid-read. There is no control to find and nothing to configure.

## Why the device decides

A document is read far more often than it is written, on screens the author never saw: a
phone in bright sun, a wide monitor at night, a room lit only by the screen. The reader has
already answered that question once, in their operating system, and every other page they
read that day honours it. A per-site picker asks them to answer it again, in a place only
this site keeps it, and then disagrees with the rest of their screen.

The text below is here to look right under both.

## A paragraph of running prose

The gap between a comfortable read and an uncomfortable one is small and entirely mechanical:
the size of the type, the length of the line, and the contrast of the page. Set the measure
too wide and the eye loses the start of the next line; too narrow and the rhythm breaks every
few words. Two palettes cover the two conditions that matter, a lit room and a dark one, and each
is tuned to clear its contrast floors rather than offered as a third of a slider. None of this
is decoration. It is the difference between finishing the chapter and closing the tab.

## A figure

![A calm reading column.](fit-a.png){#fig-reading}

@fig-reading should keep its caption and numbering under both palettes.

## Some code and a little math

```python
def comfortable(width_ch: int) -> bool:
    return 45 <= width_ch <= 75
```

Inline math like $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ and a display equation should both stay crisp under
both palettes:

$$ \text{measure} \approx \frac{\text{line length}}{\text{character width}} $$

## A callout

::: {.callout-note}
Both palettes ship in every page and one is selected at paint time from the device, so this
costs no rebuild and no round trip. It writes nothing back to the `.tmd`, adds no output
format, and works offline, so it sits cleanly inside Taliesin's invariants.
:::
